WEBVTT

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:09.720
America has a new spacecraft. Its name is Orion. It will take us beyond Earth orbit for

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:15.060
the first time in a generation. It will be able to travel farther out into our solar

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:23.050
system than we have ever gone before, allowing humankind to extend its reach to new and exciting

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:30.000
places. Orion is not a blueprint or an idea. It is

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:37.570
Across the United States, people are building, testing, and getting ready for Orion's first

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:41.260
flight test into space.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.970
Creating a spaceship is hard work.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:52.850
Work that satisfies a desire to lead, a desire to achieve, and a desire to explore.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:20.890
factory deep in southern Louisiana. Workers are cutting metal, welding, and

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:27.110
building the first Orion vehicle that will fly into space. The flight is called

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:30.000
Exploration Flight Test 1 and it will launch

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.370
an uncrewed Orion vehicle into Earth orbit.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:36.170
Orion has a big job.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:40.410
Its design allows for missions deeper into our solar system

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.580
than we have ever gone before.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:44.580
In NASA's history, we've designed spacecraft

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.900
for sometimes very specific missions,

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:48.500
like Mercury and Gemini.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:50.580
With Orion, we've designed and developed it

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.180
from the beginning with exploration in mind.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.540
It can go to the Moon, Mars, an asteroid,

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:57.380
take our crews and bring them home safely.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.830
So Orion will be able to do all of those things.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:03.790
As engineers prepare to test Orion in space,

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:07.360
other tests also put the vehicle through its paces,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.300
on land, in the water, and in the air.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.300
In Colorado, engineers conduct acoustic tests,

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.000
blasting Orion with a wall of sound

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.140
that simulates the extreme noise and vibration

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.980
experienced during a launch.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:28.220
More than 600 instruments test the durability

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.960
of Orion's avionics,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.230
propulsion, and crew life support.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:44.470
Orion will land in the ocean, and at the Langley Research Center in Virginia, a 22,000-pound

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:50.980
Orion test article undergoes a series of drop tests to certify the vehicle for water landings.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:59.450
The drop velocities differ from test to test, representing different landing positions and

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:00.000
conditions.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.800
Orion might face in the Pacific Ocean.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:07.840
Some tests even turn the vehicle upside down, deliberately.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.860
It's all a part of the test.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:16.260
Flying at 25,000 feet, a drop test article

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.900
that mimics the Orion parachute compartment

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.940
is released from a C-130 aircraft.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.920
One of many tests for the Orion parachute system,

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.670
the parachutes slow the speed of the drop test article

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:36.210
25 feet per second as it comes to a stop on the sands of the Arizona desert.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:41.530
The lessons learned during Orion's parachute development are also being applied by NASA's

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:53.510
commercial partners as they design low-Earth orbit spacecraft.

00:03:53.510 --> 00:04:00.000
For safety, Orion has its own launch abort system, a 35-foot tower which can pull the

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.080
spacecraft away from danger if there are problems with the rocket it sits upon

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:09.960
before or during the climb to space. This test of the launch abort system, called

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:15.250
Pad Abort 1, simulates an escape while still sitting on the launch pad.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:21.380
One, launch, launch, launch. Too fast for normal cameras to observe, the test is

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:30.000
really only visible in slow motion. There's fire, applying 500,000

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:37.190
pounds of thrust, accelerating from 0 to 450 miles per hour in the first two seconds of

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:38.590
flight.

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:44.260
This is the kind of speed needed to escape the top of a speeding rocket.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:50.010
Simultaneously, another set of motors is designed to do the steering, keeping the vehicle clear

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:55.610
of any danger, and positioning the craft for a parachuted landing.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:05:00.000
Finally, the launch abort system jettisons itself.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:05.760
completing the first flight test of this important safety feature.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:11.780
Engineers have gathered data from this flight and prepare for ascent abort tests, which

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:17.880
will fire the launch abort system while on a moving rocket, a tougher and higher fidelity

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.360
workout for the system.

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:33.330
For all the important testing that takes place on Earth,

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:35.510
the ultimate test for Orion

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:39.320
will be in the unique environment of space.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:43.070
Exploration Flight Test 1 will put critical Orion systems to the test

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:44.970
in the real flight environments.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.490
We'll see if Orion's design performs the way we expect,

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:50.840
and by doing that, it helps us correct any issues we find now

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:53.810
which will avoid costly changes to the design in the future.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:56.200
So it's a really smart investment to add a flight like this

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.970
early in the development process.

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:00.000
Exploration Flight Test 1,

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:01.970
will launch an uncrewed Orion

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:03.980
test spacecraft on a heavy

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:06.060
lift rocket. The launch abort system

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:07.420
will separate from the craft.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:10.640
Then Orion will go for two orbits

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:13.310
around the Earth. While in space,

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:15.110
critical systems in the vehicle

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:16.670
will be put to the test,

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:19.090
both validating and influencing

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:20.170
design decisions.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:23.040
The test continues with a high-speed

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:24.780
re-entry, rivaling

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:26.800
conditions a spacecraft would experience

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:28.180
returning from the moon

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:30.000
and giving engineers

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:36.670
data about this phase of flight. Finally, the spacecraft's parachute system will be

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:41.730
deployed for a landing in the ocean, where recovery teams will get a chance to test their

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:52.930
techniques and equipment. For Orion, the tests will continue and the work is underway. People

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:59.700
across the nation are pouring their skills, knowledge, and enthusiasm for exploration

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.110
into the development of America's next spacecraft.

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:08.190
So it's an incredibly exciting time to be working on the Orion project.

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:12.070
Of course, we're building the spacecraft that's going to take us further than we've ever gone.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:17.120
And the team that's working on it is extremely talented and dedicated, both from NASA and the industry team.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.120
Human exploration of space is a dream we all believe in.

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:24.070
And we're turning our dreams into reality with the development of Orion.
